## 2.3 Material characterizations
The surface morphologies and compositions of the samples were characterized by field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM, HITACHI, S-4800) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEM 2010 JEOL). X-ray diffraction pattern was characterized by powder X-ray diffraction (XRD, Holland Panalytical PRO PW3040/60) equipped with Cu Ka radiation ( $\lambda{=}0.154~\mathrm{nm}$ , $\mathrm{V}{=}30\ \mathrm{kV}$ , $_{\mathrm{I}=25}$ mA, $2\uptheta{=}20^{\circ}\sim90^{\circ}$ . The chemical information of samples was investigated by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Thermo ESCALAB 250XI) equipped with a monochromatic Al Ka X-ray source.
## 2.4 Electrochemical measurements
$10~\mathrm{mg}$ catalyst powder was dispersed in $950~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ DMF and sonicated for $10\mathrm{min}$ . Then, $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ 5 wt% Nafion employed as the binder was added into the above mixture and sonicated for $10~\mathrm{min}$ . Sequentially, a certain amount of the obtained catalyst suspension was transferred onto a glassy carbon electrode (GCE, diameter of 3 mm, ${\sim}0.07~\mathrm{cm}^{2},$ polished previously and dried at the room temperature, yielding a mass loading of $0.2~\mathrm{mg~cm^{-2}}$ . The modified GCE was also employed as working electrode for electrochemical measurement. In addition, a certain amount of catalyst ink was pasted onto a carbon cloth $(\sim2\mathrm{\cm}^{2}$ , precleaned by sonication in diluted HCl, deionized water, and ethanol for 15 min in turn) and dried at the room temperature with a mass loading of $1\ \mathrm{mg\cm^{-2}}$ , which was employed for the stability test.
An electrochemical workstation (Autolab M204) with a three-electrode system was employed to characterize the electrochemical properties, where a modified GCE, a Pt foil and a saturated Ag/AgCl electrode were applied as the working electrode, counter electrode and reference electrode, respectively. $0.5{\mathrm{~M~}}$ ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ and 1 M KOH were used as electrolytes. In order to evaluate the potential versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE), the potential against the saturated Ag/AgCl electrode was calibrated by the following equation: ERHE=EAg/AgCl + $0.1976\mathrm{~+~}0.059^{\ast}\mathrm{pH}$ [57]. The linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) was carried out at a scan rate of $2\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}.$ , when the Tafel plots were calculated by LSV results. At the overpotential of $250~\mathrm{\mV}$ , the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was examined in the frequency region from $10^{5}$ to $10^{-1}$ Hz with the amplitude of $5~\mathrm{mV}$ [58]. Electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) was evaluated by the double-layer capacitance $\mathrm{(C_{dl})}$ , which was derived from a series of cyclic voltammetry (CV) tests at different scan rates in non-faradic region (0.1- 0.2 V vs RHE) [59]. Mott-Schottky (M-S) analysis was performed in the potential region from $^{-1}$ to $0\mathrm{~V~}$ vs RHE with the test frequency of 1000 Hz [60]. To investigate the stability of catalysts, the LSV measurement was conducted again after CV testing for 3000 cycles continuously in the non-Faradaic region [24]. Moreover, potentiostatic measurement at a constant cathodic potential was performed in both aqueous solutions. According to the reported method [61], all electrochemical measurements were compensated for the iR drop in the tests (all data in this work were corrected if there is no specific expression).
## 3. Results and Discussion
Fig. 1 illustrates the complete synthesis route of the $\mathrm{Co/Co_{2}P/\mathrm{\it{a}C}}$ microspheres as Mott-Schottky electrocatalysts derived from Co-MOF by gradual phosphorization. Firstly, the Co-MOF was synthesized using cobalt nitrate and BTC (organic ligand) under solvothermal condition. Sequentially, the obtained Co-MOF was annealed to form $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}$ microspheres under $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere at high temperature while the organic ligand of MOF was decomposed. Then, after reacting with $\mathrm{PH}_{3}$ flow released by pyrolysis of $\mathrm{NaH}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{2}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , the metallic content in $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}$ microspheres was gradually transformed into metal phosphide $(\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P})$ and the metal/semiconductor $(\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}@\mathrm{C})$ heterostructure was successfully obtained. Meanwhile, the carbon shell transformed from organic ligand of Co-MOF was formed on the surface [54].
FESEM images were used to investigate the surface structures and morphologies of the as-prepared samples. As shown in Fig. S1, the Co-MOF microspheres display smooth and regular spherical structure with a diameter range of $0.8\sim1.2~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . After annealing at $900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , the obtained carbon-incorporated Co microspheres derived from Co-MOF preserve the regular spherical structure (Fig. 2a). After reacting with $\mathrm{PH}_{3}$ , the inner metallic Co was transformed into phosphide gradually while the sphere structure did not collapse. ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ presents a microsphere structure assembled with small particles, as shown in Fig. 2b. Elemental mapping images (Fig. 2c) further confirm that the existence and well-dispersion of C, Co and P elements throughout ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ microspheres. Meanwhile, the TEM characterization of ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ in Fig. 2d further proves the uniform microsphere structure constructed by small particles and carbon shell with a mean diameter around $0.8~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , which is in accordance with FESEM image. High-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) image (Fig. 2e) shows two different interlaced lattice fringes with the interplanar spacings of 0.205 nm and $0.221\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which can be ascribed to (111) plane of metallic Co phase and (121) plane of semi-conductive $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ phase, respectively. The existence of carbon shell $(3\sim100\ \mathrm{nm})$ on the surface of microspheres can be observed clearly from HRTEM in Fig. 2d and Fig. S2. The carbon layer implanted on the microsphere plays a vital role in facilitating the electrons transfer and enhancing the structural stability in catalytic process [55].
Fig. 3a presents the XRD patterns of all as-prepared samples. Co@C displays three diffraction peaks at $44.2^{\circ}$ , $51.5^{\circ}$ and $75.8^{\circ}$ , which are correlated with (111), (200), (220) planes of metallic Co (JCPDS No. 15-0806). The typical peaks of metallic Co can be still observed obviously in ${\mathrm{Co/Co_{2}P/\mathrm{\it{a}}C}}$ samples after phosphorization, proving the existence of the metallic Co phase in ${\mathrm{Co/Co_{2}P/\mathrm{\it{a}}\mathrm{\mathrm{C}}}}$ . The peaks at 40.7o, $43.3^{\mathrm{o}}$ and $48.7^{\circ}$ in ${\mathrm{Co/Co_{2}P/\mathrm{\it{a}}C}}$ can be indexed to (121), (211) and (031) planes of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ (JCPDS No. 32-0306) respectively, corresponding to the
HRTEM characterization (Fig. 2e). What’s more, diffraction peaks of Co phase in ${\mathrm{Co/Co_{2}P/\mathrm{\it{a}}C}}$ samples become weaker with the increase of $\mathrm{NaH}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{2}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ used in the phosphorization process while characteristic peaks of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ appear in ${\mathrm{Co}}/{\mathrm{Co}}_{2}{\mathrm{P}}\mathcal{Q}{\mathrm{C}}{-}5$ firstly and exist stably in other ${\mathrm{Co/Co_{2}P/\mathrm{\it{a}}C}}$ samples, indicating that the phosphorization degree can be efficiently controlled via changing the mass ratio of $\mathrm{NaH}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{2}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}$ . When the mass ratio of precursors was increased to 20:1, no characteristic peaks of metallic Co can be observed, indicating the complete conversion of $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}$ into ${\mathrm{Co}}_{2}{\mathrm{P}}@{\mathrm{C}}.$
