To investigate the chemical information of the as-obtained samples, XPS measurement of ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ was conducted. The coexistence of Co, C, O and $\mathrm{\bfP}$ elements is further confirmed by the survey spectrum in Fig. 3b. The high-resolution XPS spectra depict the elements Co and P in ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ , as shown in Fig. 3c and 3d. The Co 2p XPS spectrum can be fitted into two doublets, characteristic of $\mathrm{Co}~2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}$ for 797.3 eV and $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ for $798.8\ \mathrm{eV},$ ) and Co $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ ( $\mathrm{Co}^{3+}$ for $781.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ for $783.2\ \mathrm{eV})$ , both of which shift slightly to higher binding energy [62]. Two typical peaks of metallic Co in ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ are found at 778.6 $\mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{Co}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $793.6\mathrm{eV}$ for Co $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ [63]. In addition, the remaining two peaks centered at $785.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $802.1{\mathrm{~eV}}$ are related to two satellite peaks of Co element [33]. In P 2p XPS spectrum (Fig. 3d), two peaks appearing at 129.6 and $130.2~\mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to $\mathrm{~P~}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $\textsf{P}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ in metal phosphide while a broad peak centering at $133.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ is attributed to the formation of P-O bond due to the surface oxidation of phosphide species [37, 64, 65], suggesting the successful phosphorization of carbon coated $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}$ microspheres. The XPS result further illustrates the existence of metallic Co and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ in ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10.$ -
Electrocatalytic performances of the samples were investigated in $\Nu_{2}$ -saturated $0.5{\bf M}$ ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ electrolyte by a three-electrode system with iR compensation. Fig. 4a exhibits the polarization curves of all catalysts. According to the previous research [66], the bare GCE does not exhibit HER activity. Compared with the pristine $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}$ microspheres, significant improvement in HER performance has been found for ${\mathrm{Co/Co_{2}P/\mathrm{\it{a}}C}}$ microspheres due to the strong synergistic effect between metallic Co and semi-conductive $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ as double catalyst for HER. It is obvious that ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ presents the optimal HER activity with an overpotential of $192\mathrm{mV}(\eta_{10})$ to deliver a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , while those of $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}\mathscr{Q}\mathrm{C}{\preceq}$ , $_{\mathrm{Co/Co_{2}P\mathcal{Q}C-15}}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}@\mathrm{C}$ are $220\mathrm{mV}$ , 208 mV and $212~\mathrm{\mV}$ , respectively. Furthermore, ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ maintains superior electrocatalytic activity even at the higher applied potential.
To explore the HER kinetics, the Tafel plots were obtained from the polarization curves. The Tafel slope values of all samples are listed in Fig. 4b and Table 1. All the Tafel slopes fall within the range of $38{\sim}118~\mathrm{mV}$ dec-1, which suggests the Volmer-Heyrovsky process is the main reaction pathway for HER process [36]. According to the pioneering literatures [67-69], the negative charged $\mathrm{\bfP}$ attracts protons via strong electrostatic affinity and transforms them into $\mathrm{H}$ intermediates, which accelerates the adsorption step (Volmer) dramatically. Generally, the reaction rate of Volmer step is much faster than that of Heyrovsky step in acidic media, and thus the whole HER reaction is kinetically controlled by the Heyrovsky step [59]. It can be seen that the Tafel slopes of ${\mathrm{Co/Co_{2}P/\mathrm{\it{a}}C}}$ decrease greatly compared with $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}$ due to the formation of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ and ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ exhibits the optimal Tafel slope. The enhanced HER kinetics of ${\mathrm{Co/Co_{2}P/\mathrm{\it{a}}C}}$ can be attributed to the synergetic effect of metallic Co and semi-conductive $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ . $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ with more unsaturated coordination atoms on the surface can capture abundant electrons in the Volmer reaction and transfer electrons efficiently through the Mott-Schottky interface (as shown in Fig. 1). However, when the content of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ further increases, the samples show inferior kinetics, indicating that Mott-Schottky effect is affected by the ratio of metallic Co to semi-conductive Co2P in the electrocatalyst [36].
