Electrical conductivity is regarded as a major factor that influence electrocatalyst performance of HER. The Nyquist plots are fitted with the equivalent electric circuit model as shown in Fig. 4c. All Nyquist plots include a semicircle in high frequency region related to the charge-transfer resistance $\mathrm{(R_{ct})}$ and an inclined line in low frequency region which reflects the ion diffusion process [70]. The fitted $\mathrm{R_{ct}}$ values of Co@C, Co/Co2P@C-5, Co/Co2P@C-10, $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}\mathscr{Q}\mathrm{C}{-}15$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}@{\mathrm{C}}$ are 5.86 Ω, 6.77 Ω, 4.46 Ω, 6.49 Ω and $7.52\ \Omega$ , respectively. Obviously, the smaller $\mathrm{R_{ct}}$ reveals that the Mott-Schottky interface has played a significant role in facilitating charge transfer [24]. Furthermore, ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ possesses the smallest $\mathrm{\sfR}_{\mathrm{ct}},$ indicating the expedited electron transfer and redox reaction at the interface, which is beneficial to the HER performance in acidic media. To further investigate the detailed reasons for the enhanced HER activity, ECSA was assessed by the value of the electrochemical double-layer capacitance $\mathrm{(C_{dl})}$ . CV test was conducted repetitively between 0.1 and $0.2\mathrm{~V~}$ versus RHE at different scan rates (Fig. S3). As displayed in Fig. 4d and Table 1, the $\mathrm{C_{dl}}$ of Co/Co2P@C-10 $\mathrm{0.40~mF~}\mathrm{cm^{-2}})$ is greatly higher than those of other samples, demonstrating that ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ microspheres possess the highest ECSA to offer more active sites for HER. The optimal $\mathrm{R_{ct}}$ and
ECSA of ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ can be attributed to the optimal mass radio of metallic Co and semi-conductive $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ in Mott-Schottky electrocatalyst [55].
To evaluate the catalytic behavior of the samples in wide-pH environment, it’s necessary to test their HER activity in $\Nu_{2}$ -saturated 1 M KOH electrolyte. Fig. 5a presents the iR-corrected polarization curves of all samples. Similar to the acidic media, all the ${\mathrm{Co/Co_{2}P}}\mathcal{Q}\mathrm{C}$ samples exhibit higher HER activity than pristine $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}$ microspheres after the phosphorization treatment. Co/Co2P@C-10 microspheres achieve a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at an overpotential of $158~\mathrm{mV}$ , better than of those of ${\mathrm{Co}}/{\mathrm{Co}}_{2}{\mathrm{P}}\mathcal{Q}{\mathrm{C}}{-}5$ (207 mV), Co/Co2P@C-15 ( $193\ \mathrm{\mV})$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}@\mathrm{C}$ ( $197~\mathrm{mV},$ . More surprisingly, the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\Pi}}$ values of all samples in alkaline environment is smaller than those in acidic environment, demonstrating the more excellent electrocatalytic activity in alkaline electrolyte. To analyze the electrocatalyst activity, fitted Tafel slopes (Fig. 5b), EIS (Fig. 5c) and ECSA (Fig. 5d and Fig. S4) were also measured and the results are listed in Table 2. It can be observed that ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ exhibits excellent HER activity in alkaline electrolyte due to its promoted reaction kinetics, fast charge transfer and largest ECSA. Furthermore, the HER performance of ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ as a Mott-Schottky electrocatalyst is better than those of most representative non-precious electrocatalysts reported in the literatures under wide-pH environment (Table S1 and Table S2 in Supporting Information).
