Electrocatalytic properties of ${\mathrm{Co}}\@circled{a}{\mathrm{C}},$ , Co/Co2P@C-X and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}@\mathrm{C}$ in alkaline electrolyte.
Considering the semi-conductive property of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ , Mott-Schottky (M-S) analysis of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}@\mathrm{C}$ and all $_\mathrm{Co/Co_{2}P\mathcal{Q}C-X}$ samples was performed to clarify the electrons transfer caused by formation of the integrated Mott-Schottky heterojunction. There exists a negative correlation between the slope in M-S plot and $\mathrm{N_{d}}\ [60]$ , which indicates that the smaller slope represents the increasing charge carrier density. As shown in the Fig. 6a and Fig. 6b, the positive slopes confirm that the carrier is electron [71]. Meanwhile, the slopes of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ in M-S plots have decreased obviously after incorporating with metallic Co in both electrolytes and the slopes of ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ reach minimum value representing the maximal carrier density, which verifies that Mott-Schottky heterojunction accelerates the electron transfer dramatically. The result of M-S measurement is consistent with the electrocatalytic analyses.
Moreover, electrocatalyst durability is one of the critical parameters for the practical application. Electrocatalytic stability of ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ was examined by two methods, including accelerated degradation test and chronopotentiometry test in both two electrolytes (using modified CFP as the working electrode in two methods, catalyst loading: ${\sim}1~\mathrm{mg~cm^{-2}}$ ). CV cycles between 0.05 and 0.25 V versus RHE were conducted repetitively. After 3000 CV cycles, the polarization curve was measured again in the same condition and it can be found that the ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ microspheres can still perform efficient HER activity (Inset images in Fig. 7a and Fig. 7b). The $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{n}}_{10}$ of ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ in acidic and alkaline electrolytes only increased 15 and $35~\mathrm{\mV}$ , respectively. Moreover, potentiostatic electrolysis measurement was also repeated at a constant overpotential of $200~\mathrm{mV}$ to assess the stability for three times. With continuous evolution of robust hydrogen gas, the current density had been maintained for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ with little decay as shown in Fig. 7a and Fig. 7b. The current density remains $83.26\%$ and $82.13\%$ of original values in acidic and alkaline electrolytes, respectively (Fig. S5). The FESEM and TEM images of the post-HER ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ after potentiostatic measurement are presented in Fig. 7c, Fig. 7d and Fig. 7e, confirming that the microsphere structure of ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ does not collapse after the long-term stability test. In Fig. 7f, two different interlaced lattice fringes with the interplanar spacings of $0.205~\mathrm{nm}$ and $0.221\ \mathrm{nm}$ ascribed to (111) plane of metallic Co phase and (121) plane of semi-conductive $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ phase still can be observed clearly. Mott-Schottky heterojunction does not disappear after the long-term electrolysis test. All observation indicates the satisfied electrocatalyst durability in wide-pH environment which benefits from rapid electron transfer caused by Mott-Schottky interface, porous carbon shell supporting and spherical structure.
Fig. 7 Evaluation of HER catalytic durability for ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ in both electrolytes: potentiostatic curves of Co/Co2P@C-10 in (a) acidic electrolyte and (b) alkaline electrolyte (Insert: LSV curves before and after 3000 CV cycles). FESEM images (c, d), TEM image (e) and HRTEM image (f) of ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ after the potentiostatic test.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, ${\mathrm{Co/Co_{2}P/\mathrm{\it{a}}C}}$ microspheres used as Mott-Schottky electrocatalysts were fabricated from Co-MOF precursor via solvothermal reaction with subsequent carbonization and gradual phosphorization process. The optimized ${\bf C o}/{\bf C o}_{2}{\bf P}\varpi{\bf C}{\bf-}10$ microspheres exhibit excellent HER performance in wide-pH operational condition, especially in alkaline electrolyte (10 mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $158~\mathrm{\mV})$ , the overpotential of which is much less than reported works, such as CoP (10 mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $209~\mathrm{mV}$ ) [57] and $\mathrm{CoO_{x}}$ ( $\mathrm{10\mA\cm^{-2}}$ at $232~\mathrm{mV}.$ ) [72]. The favorable catalytic property of Co/Co2P@C Mott-Schottky electrocatalysts is ascribed to the following factors (i) high electrical conductivity benefiting from Mott-Schottky effect at the heterointerface, (ii) synergy effect of metallic Co and semiconductor $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ as double electrocatalyst and (iii) porous carbon shell. Although the HER activity of ${\mathrm{Co/Co_{2}P/\mathrm{\it{a}}C}}$ is improved, the overpotential is still high for the practical application, which should be further investigated in the future. The strategy in this work will offer a novel method to explore metal/semiconductor hybrids as Mott–Schottky electrocatalysts.
